@@s001 Cache and coherence: the MESI protocol
@@s002 <span>Week 9 · Performance</span>
@@s003 Cache and coherence: the MESI protocol
@@s004 The CPU is faster than the RAM, and the cache closes the gap. With several cores each cache has a copy of the data, and the copies must agree: that is the job of the MESI protocol.
@@s005 The bridge between CPU and RAM
@@s006 The CPU works much faster than the RAM. The <b>cache</b> is a small, fast memory, close to the CPU, that keeps copies of the data used recently. It works well because of <b>locality of access</b>: if a datum is needed now, it will probably be needed again, and data close together in memory are often needed together.
@@s007 A read from memory
@@s008 dato:requested address,ora:step in progress,ok:fast,no:slow
@@s009 The first step is always to look for it in the cache.
@@s010 This is the good case: the datum is already there, and the RAM is not touched.
@@s011 The cache copies a whole block, not a single byte: so the nearby data are already ready.
@@s012 More cores, more caches
@@s013 Each core has its own caches. The same memory address can therefore be in several caches at the same time. The block of memory that a cache copies is called a <b>cache line</b>: usually 64 bytes. The caches must agree on the value of each line: the mechanism is called a <b>coherence protocol</b>, and in the slides it is <b>MESI</b>, from the name of its four states.
@@s014 The MESI states
@@s015 Each cache line, in each core, is in one of four states:
@@s016 state
@@s017 meaning
@@s018 is the RAM up to date?
@@s019 <b>M</b> Modified
@@s020 modified copy, present only in this cache
@@s021 no: the RAM has the old value
@@s022 <b>E</b> Exclusive
@@s023 unmodified copy, present only here
@@s024 yes
@@s025 <b>S</b> Shared
@@s026 unmodified copy, present in several caches
@@s027 yes
@@s028 <b>I</b> Invalid
@@s029 the line is not valid: it must be read again
@@s030 To write to a line in state S, the core must first send an <b>invalidate</b> message to the other caches, and wait for their confirmation (<i>ack</i>). Only then does the line become M.
@@s031 Fast or slow
@@s032 The speed depends on what the core wants to do and on the state of the line in its cache. The most important rows of the slide:
@@s033 current state
@@s034 the core wants
@@s035 speed
@@s036 read or write
@@s037 fast: the datum is already its own
@@s038 read
@@s039 fast: the copy is valid
@@s040 read
@@s041 slow: it must be taken from another cache or from the RAM
@@s042 write
@@s043 very slow: invalidate all the other copies
@@s044 write
@@s045 very slow: first read, then invalidate
@@s046 Two cores on the same line
@@s047 dato:value,ora:what changes,ok:stable state,no:high cost
@@s048 Nobody else has it: the copy is its own and the RAM is up to date.
@@s049 Two identical and clean copies: fast reads for both.
@@s050 Writing on S costs: the invalidate message must reach the other core.
@@s051 Every exchange of writes and reads between two cores costs a trip on the bus: the ping-pong of the next page.
@@s052 What follows for the code
@@s053 The idea
@@s054 The cost is not in the computation, it is in the movement of the lines between caches. A datum written only by one thread stays in M in its cache: fast. A datum written by several threads goes back and forth: slow.
@@s055 If you come from…
@@s056 If you have used only threads without thinking about memory, this is the part you are missing: there is not one single "value" in memory, there are copies in several caches that must agree.
